window.BioChecks[28] = {"2007-Q13a":[{"q":"Which blood vessel brings blood back to the heart from the lungs?","right":"Pulmonary vein","wrong":["Pulmonary artery","Vena cava","Aorta"],"why":"The pulmonary vein carries oxygen-rich blood from the lungs to the heart; the pulmonary artery goes the other way."},{"q":"Which gas does the pulmonary vein mainly carry, and how?","right":"Oxygen, joined to haemoglobin in red cells","wrong":["Carbon dioxide, as hydrogencarbonate","Oxygen, carried by white blood cells","Carbon dioxide, joined to haemoglobin"],"why":"Blood leaving the lungs is full of oxygen, which binds to haemoglobin in red blood cells as oxyhaemoglobin."}],"2007-Q13b":[{"q":"On your diagram, which tube branches off the trachea into each lung?","right":"Bronchus","wrong":["Bronchiole","Oesophagus","Larynx"],"why":"The trachea splits into two bronchi, one for each lung; bronchioles are the smaller branches inside the lung."},{"q":"What are the functions of the epiglottis and the larynx? (2 points)","right":"Epiglottis closes off the trachea; larynx makes sound","wrong":["Epiglottis makes sound; larynx closes off the trachea","Epiglottis closes off the oesophagus; larynx makes sound","Epiglottis closes off the trachea; larynx pumps air in"],"why":"Two points: the epiglottis closes off the trachea when you swallow, and the larynx (voice box) makes sound."},{"q":"In inhalation, what happens to the chest's volume and the air pressure inside? (2 of 4 points)","right":"Volume increases, pressure decreases","wrong":["Volume decreases, pressure increases","Volume increases, pressure increases","Volume decreases, pressure decreases"],"why":"4 points: chest volume up and pressure down (both needed), plus two of: diaphragm contracts and lowers, intercostals contract, ribs up, air in."}],"2007-Q13c":[{"q":"Give three ways an alveolus is adapted for efficient gas exchange. (3 points)","right":"Thin walls, moist surface, large surface area","wrong":["Thick walls, moist surface, large surface area","Thin walls, dry waxy lining, large surface area","Thin walls, moist surface, valves to stop backflow"],"why":"Three of: thin walls, a moist surface, a large surface area, a capillary network around it, and elastic walls."},{"q":"By what process do gases pass between the alveolus and the blood?","right":"Diffusion","wrong":["Osmosis","Active transport","Respiration"],"why":"Gases move from high to low concentration with no energy needed: that's diffusion. Osmosis is only for water."},{"q":"Which of these is a disorder of the breathing system?","right":"Asthma","wrong":["Anaemia","Angina","Arthritis"],"why":"Asthma affects the airways, so it's a breathing disorder; anaemia is a blood disorder and angina a heart one."},{"q":"In asthma, what causes the difficulty in breathing?","right":"Bronchioles narrow, so less air gets through","wrong":["Alveolar walls break down and merge","Cartilage rings in the trachea collapse","The diaphragm stops contracting"],"why":"Triggers like dust or pet dander make the bronchioles tighten and narrow, so air struggles to get through."}],"2009-Q13c":[{"q":"Which part is labelled A on the diagram?","right":"Bronchiole","wrong":["Bronchus","Alveolus","Capillary"],"why":"A is the narrow airway leading into the air sacs: a bronchiole. A bronchus is a much wider tube."},{"q":"Give two features shown on the diagram that help gas exchange. (2 points)","right":"Thin walls; capillaries pressed close to alveoli","wrong":["Thick walls; capillaries pressed close to alveoli","Thin walls; a dry inner lining in the alveoli","Large surface area; capillaries with valves"],"why":"Two of: thin walls, moist surfaces, capillaries close to the alveoli, a large surface area, and large numbers of alveoli."},{"q":"Name a breathing disorder and a way to prevent it. (2 of 4 points)","right":"Asthma: avoid allergens such as dust and pet hair","wrong":["Asthma: take antibiotics every few weeks","Asthma: eat plenty of iron-rich food","Asthma: breathe through the mouth, not nose"],"why":"Four points: name it (asthma), a cause (allergens like dust), prevention (avoid allergens) and a treatment (an inhaler)."},{"q":"A rise in which dissolved gas makes you breathe deeper or faster?","right":"Carbon dioxide","wrong":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Water vapour"],"why":"A rise in CO2 in the blood is sensed by the brain, which speeds up breathing to get rid of it."}],"2010-Q7a":[{"q":"Which body cavity holds the heart and lungs?","right":"Thoracic (chest) cavity","wrong":["Abdominal cavity","Pelvic cavity","Cranial cavity"],"why":"The heart and lungs sit in the thorax (chest), above the diaphragm; the abdomen is below it."}],"2011-Q8b":[{"q":"Why do heart rate and breathing rate rise during exercise? (2 points)","right":"More O2 and energy needed; more CO2 to remove","wrong":["More CO2 and energy needed; more O2 to remove","More O2 and energy needed; less CO2 made","More glucose absorbed in lungs; more O2 to remove"],"why":"Two points: muscles need more oxygen and energy, and make more CO2 to get rid of."}],"2012-Q15c":[{"q":"How do the lungs help with homeostasis?","right":"They excrete carbon dioxide","wrong":["They excrete urea","They store glycogen","They produce insulin"],"why":"The lungs get rid of CO2 (and some water and heat), keeping blood CO2 steady. Urea goes out via the kidneys."}],"2014-Q9b":[{"q":"In the exercise investigation, what did you measure first?","right":"The resting pulse or breathing rate","wrong":["The rate during hard exercise","The recovery time after exercise","The person's body temperature"],"why":"You first find the rate at rest, so you have a baseline to compare with the rates after exercise."},{"q":"How do you measure pulse rate?","right":"Feel the pulse and count beats in a set time","wrong":["Feel the pulse and judge how strong it is","Count beats until the pulse slows down","Time how long you can hold your breath"],"why":"Find the pulse (e.g. at the wrist) and count the beats for a set time, like 60 s, to get beats per minute."}],"2014-Q12b":[{"q":"On your diagram, in what order does air pass through the breathing tract?","right":"Trachea, bronchus, bronchiole, alveolus","wrong":["Trachea, bronchiole, bronchus, alveolus","Bronchus, trachea, bronchiole, alveolus","Trachea, alveolus, bronchus, bronchiole"],"why":"The trachea splits into two bronchi, which branch into smaller bronchioles that end in alveoli."},{"q":"What starts the process of inhalation? (one of 6 points)","right":"The brain sends impulses to breathing muscles","wrong":["The lungs expand on their own","The heart sends impulses to the diaphragm","Air rushes in and pushes the ribs out"],"why":"Any 6: brain signals the muscles; intercostals contract; diaphragm contracts; ribs up and out; diaphragm down; volume up; pressure down; air in."}],"2014-Q12c":[{"q":"In which part of a cell is carbon dioxide made?","right":"Mitochondrion","wrong":["Ribosome","Chloroplast","Nucleus"],"why":"CO2 is released in stage 2 of aerobic respiration (the Krebs cycle), which happens in the mitochondrion."},{"q":"Which feature of a capillary helps it take up CO2 quickly?","right":"Its wall is only one cell thick","wrong":["Its wall is thick and muscular","It has valves along its length","It has a very wide lumen"],"why":"A wall one cell thick gives CO2 a very short distance to diffuse. Valves and wide lumens belong to veins."},{"q":"Why does venous blood usually carry more CO2 than arterial blood?","right":"It has collected CO2 from respiring cells","wrong":["It picked up CO2 from air in the lungs","Its oxygen has turned into CO2","Veins have valves that trap the CO2"],"why":"Body cells release CO2 from respiration into the blood; arterial blood was cleared of CO2 in the lungs."},{"q":"Name a blood vessel that is an exception, and give a reason. (2 points)","right":"Pulmonary vein: its CO2 was just removed in the lungs","wrong":["Vena cava: its CO2 was just removed in the lungs","Pulmonary vein: its blood picks up CO2 in the lungs","Renal vein: its CO2 was all removed by the kidneys"],"why":"Two points: the pulmonary vein, as its blood has just been cleared of CO2 in the lungs. (The pulmonary artery is the other exception.)"},{"q":"What role does CO2 play in controlling breathing rate? (2 points)","right":"Medulla oblongata senses CO2; more CO2 speeds up breathing","wrong":["Medulla oblongata senses CO2; more CO2 slows down breathing","Cerebellum senses CO2; more CO2 speeds up breathing","Medulla oblongata senses CO2; less CO2 speeds up breathing"],"why":"Two points: the medulla oblongata in the brain detects the CO2 level in the blood, and more CO2 makes it speed up breathing."}],"2016-Q8b":[{"q":"What was the control in this exercise investigation?","right":"The rate measured at rest","wrong":["The rate straight after exercise","The highest rate during exercise","The time taken to recover"],"why":"The resting rate is the control: the normal rate with no exercise, to compare the other results with."},{"q":"Why do you need this control?","right":"To compare with the rates after exercise","wrong":["To warm the person up before exercise","To find the person's highest rate","To keep the rate the same throughout"],"why":"Without the resting rate, you couldn't tell how much exercise changed the pulse or breathing rate."},{"q":"How should you label the axes and draw the two curves? (3 points)","right":"x: exercise time; y: rate; B's curve rises more than A's","wrong":["x: rate; y: exercise time; B's curve rises more than A's","x: exercise time; y: rate; A's curve rises more than B's","x: exercise time; y: rate; both curves rise the same amount"],"why":"Three points: x-axis is exercise time, y-axis is pulse or breathing rate, and unfit B's curve rises more than athlete A's."},{"q":"How could you measure recovery time? (3 points)","right":"Right after exercise, count pulse per min; time till resting","wrong":["Right after exercise, count pulse per min; time to its peak","Before exercise, count pulse per min; time the exercise","During exercise, count pulse per min; time till it's steady"],"why":"Three points: start straight after exercise, count the pulse (or breaths) per minute, and time how long until it is back to the resting rate."}],"2017-Q15a":[{"q":"During inhalation, what does the diaphragm do? (one of 5 points)","right":"Contracts and flattens, moving down","wrong":["Relaxes and flattens, moving down","Contracts and domes, moving up","Relaxes and domes, moving up"],"why":"Any 5: more CO2; brain sends a message; diaphragm contracts and flattens; intercostals contract, ribs up; volume up; pressure down; air in."},{"q":"Which of these is a human breathing disorder?","right":"Bronchitis","wrong":["Hepatitis","Gastritis","Appendicitis"],"why":"Bronchitis is inflamed bronchi, the airways into the lungs; the others affect the liver, stomach and gut."},{"q":"For bronchitis, state a cause and a treatment. (2 points)","right":"Infection or irritants like smoke; treated with antibiotics","wrong":["A lack of iron in the diet; treated with antibiotics","Infection or irritants like smoke; treated with insulin","Eating too much salt; treated with iron tablets"],"why":"Two points: a cause (an infection, or irritants such as smoke) and a treatment (antibiotics for a bacterial infection)."}],"2019-Q6":[{"q":"Which blood vessel does the lung capillary branch off from?","right":"Pulmonary artery","wrong":["Pulmonary vein","Aorta","Vena cava"],"why":"Blood reaches the lungs in the pulmonary artery, which branches into arterioles and then these capillaries."},{"q":"Give three features of alveoli or their capillaries that help gas exchange. (3 points)","right":"Thin walls, large surface area, dense capillary network","wrong":["Thick walls, large surface area, dense capillary network","Thin walls, large surface area, capillaries with valves","Thin walls, dry waxy lining, dense capillary network"],"why":"Three of: thin walls, large surface area, moist surface, many alveoli, capillaries close to the alveoli in a dense network."},{"q":"Name a breathing disorder, a cause and a treatment. (3 points)","right":"Asthma; caused by allergens like dust; treat with an inhaler","wrong":["Asthma; caused by a lack of iron; treat with an inhaler","Asthma; caused by pollen or dust; treat with antibiotics","Bronchitis; caused by smoking; treat with insulin injections"],"why":"Three points: asthma, caused by allergens like dust, treated with an inhaler (or bronchitis, from infection or smoke, treated with antibiotics)."}],"2021-Q16d":[{"q":"Which structures at the end of the airways carry out gas exchange?","right":"Alveoli","wrong":["Bronchioles","Bronchi","Villi"],"why":"The airways end in millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli. Villi are the finger-like folds in the gut."},{"q":"State two adaptations of the alveoli that make gas exchange efficient. (2 points)","right":"Large surface area; thin walls","wrong":["Large surface area; thick walls","Thin walls; lined with cilia","Dry surface; rich blood supply"],"why":"Two of: a large surface area, thin walls, a moist surface, and a rich capillary supply around them."},{"q":"Which gas dissolved in plasma controls the rate of breathing?","right":"Carbon dioxide","wrong":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Carbon monoxide"],"why":"The brain mainly monitors CO2 in the plasma; more CO2 means you breathe faster to get rid of it."},{"q":"Which part of the brain detects this gas?","right":"Medulla oblongata","wrong":["Hypothalamus","Cerebellum","Cerebrum"],"why":"The medulla oblongata controls breathing and heart rate. The hypothalamus controls things like temperature."},{"q":"How does the breathing system respond to a high CO2 level?","right":"Breathing gets faster and deeper","wrong":["Breathing gets slower and shallower","Breathing gets slower but deeper","Breathing stops until CO2 falls"],"why":"Faster, deeper breaths get rid of the extra CO2 from the blood more quickly."},{"q":"Why does air flow into the lungs during inhalation? (one of 4 points)","right":"Chest pressure falls below the air outside","wrong":["Chest pressure rises above the air outside","The lungs' own muscles suck air in","The alveoli pump air in by squeezing"],"why":"Pressure falls, so air flows in. Any 4: brain signals, muscles contract, ribs up and out, diaphragm down, volume up, pressure down, air in."}],"2023-Q17c":[{"q":"What must your sketch of an alveolus show? (3 points)","right":"Alveolus and capillary; O2 arrow to blood, CO2 to alveolus","wrong":["Alveolus and capillary; CO2 arrow to blood, O2 to alveolus","Alveolus and capillary; O2 and CO2 arrows both to blood","Alveolus and capillary; O2 and CO2 arrows both to alveolus"],"why":"Three points: an alveolus with its capillary, an arrow for O2 from the alveolus into the blood, and one for CO2 from the blood into the alveolus."},{"q":"Which diagram shows inhalation, and how can you tell?","right":"X, because the ribs move up and out","wrong":["Y, because the ribs move down and in","X, because the diaphragm moves up","Y, because the diaphragm moves down"],"why":"In X the ribs move up and out and the diaphragm moves down, so the chest gets bigger: that's inhalation."}],"2025-Q16b":[{"q":"What type of blood cell is C, the one that carries a gas?","right":"Red blood cell","wrong":["White blood cell","Platelet","Lymphocyte"],"why":"Red blood cells are packed with haemoglobin, which carries oxygen; white cells like lymphocytes fight infection."},{"q":"Which feature of structure B (the alveolus) helps gas exchange?","right":"A moist inner surface","wrong":["A thick layer of muscle","A lining of beating cilia","A wall stiffened by cartilage"],"why":"Gases dissolve in the moist lining first, which lets them diffuse across the thin wall."},{"q":"Which gas is X and which is Y, judging by their directions?","right":"X is carbon dioxide (leaving the blood), Y is oxygen","wrong":["X is oxygen (leaving the blood), Y is carbon dioxide","X and Y are both oxygen, moving both ways","X and Y are both carbon dioxide, moving both ways"],"why":"Blood reaching the lungs is high in CO2, so CO2 (X) diffuses out into the alveolus. O2 (Y) diffuses the other way, into the blood."},{"q":"What is the movement of these gases called?","right":"Diffusion","wrong":["Osmosis","Active transport","Ventilation"],"why":"Each gas moves from high to low concentration across thin walls, with no energy used: that's diffusion."},{"q":"Which two muscles are directly involved in inhalation?","right":"Diaphragm and intercostal muscles","wrong":["Diaphragm and abdominal muscles","Intercostals and cardiac muscle","Diaphragm and trachea muscles"],"why":"The diaphragm and the intercostals (between the ribs) both contract to make the chest bigger."},{"q":"During exhalation, what do the breathing muscles do?","right":"Relax, so ribs move down and diaphragm moves up","wrong":["Relax, so ribs move up and diaphragm moves down","Contract, so ribs move up and diaphragm moves down","Relax, so ribs move down and diaphragm flattens"],"why":"As the muscles relax, the ribs move down and in and the diaphragm domes up, so chest volume falls and air is pushed out."},{"q":"Which disorder is correctly matched with a treatment?","right":"Bronchitis: antibiotics, if bacterial","wrong":["Asthma: a course of antibiotics","Bronchitis: insulin injections","Asthma: iron tablets"],"why":"Antibiotics kill bacteria, so they treat bacterial bronchitis; asthma is treated with an inhaler instead."}],"2026-Q3":[{"q":"In which body cavity is the breathing system found?","right":"Thoracic (chest) cavity","wrong":["Abdominal cavity","Pelvic cavity","Cranial cavity"],"why":"The lungs and most of the airways sit in the thoracic cavity, protected by the ribs, above the diaphragm."},{"q":"What does the epiglottis do?","right":"Stops food going down the trachea","wrong":["Makes sounds in the voice box","Warms and filters incoming air","Pushes food down the oesophagus"],"why":"When you swallow, the epiglottis covers the top of the trachea, so food goes down the oesophagus instead."},{"q":"What do the rings of cartilage in the trachea do?","right":"Keep the trachea open so air can pass","wrong":["Close the trachea when you swallow","Sweep mucus up towards the throat","Squeeze to push air into the lungs"],"why":"The stiff C-shaped rings stop the trachea collapsing when the pressure inside drops as you breathe in."},{"q":"Where should you draw the diaphragm on the diagram?","right":"Just under the lungs, as a dome","wrong":["Just above the lungs, near the trachea","Between the two lungs","Lower down, below the stomach"],"why":"The diaphragm is a dome-shaped sheet of muscle right under the lungs, separating the chest from the abdomen."},{"q":"Which way should your arrow point for the diaphragm in inhalation?","right":"Downwards, away from the lungs","wrong":["Upwards, into the chest","Outwards, towards the ribs","Nowhere: only the ribs move"],"why":"When the diaphragm contracts it flattens and moves down, making the chest bigger."},{"q":"Apart from the diaphragm, which muscle helps you breathe in?","right":"Intercostal muscles","wrong":["Abdominal muscles","Cardiac muscle","Muscle in the trachea wall"],"why":"The intercostal muscles between the ribs contract to lift the rib cage up and out."},{"q":"What happens to the rib cage during inhalation?","right":"It moves up and out","wrong":["It moves down and in","It moves up and in","It moves down and out"],"why":"The intercostal muscles contract and lift the ribs up and out, which makes the chest bigger."}],"S1-Q3a":[{"q":"Which way should your O2 and CO2 arrows point on the diagram?","right":"O2 into the capillary, CO2 into the air space","wrong":["O2 into the air space, CO2 into the capillary","Both gases into the capillary","Both gases into the air space"],"why":"Each gas diffuses down its gradient: O2 is high in the alveolar air, CO2 is high in the blood."},{"q":"By what process does this gas exchange happen?","right":"Diffusion","wrong":["Osmosis","Active transport","Phagocytosis"],"why":"O2 and CO2 move from high to low concentration through the thin walls, using no energy: diffusion."},{"q":"How does a thin alveolar wall help gas exchange?","right":"Gases have only a short distance to diffuse","wrong":["Gases can be pumped across using energy","It stops water vapour from escaping","It keeps the inside surface dry"],"why":"The wall is one cell thick, so O2 and CO2 only travel a tiny distance, which makes diffusion fast."}],"S1-Q3c":[{"q":"What is the main way carbon dioxide is carried in the blood?","right":"As hydrogencarbonate ions in the plasma","wrong":["Mainly bound to haemoglobin","Carried inside white blood cells","As tiny gas bubbles in the plasma"],"why":"Most CO2 reacts with water to form hydrogencarbonate ions in the plasma; only some joins haemoglobin."},{"q":"What is the main way oxygen is carried in the blood?","right":"Bound to haemoglobin in red blood cells","wrong":["Mostly dissolved in the plasma","As hydrogencarbonate ions in plasma","Carried inside white blood cells"],"why":"Oxygen joins haemoglobin in red blood cells to form oxyhaemoglobin; very little dissolves in plasma."},{"q":"What are the CO2 and O2 levels in the pulmonary artery and pulmonary vein? (2 points)","right":"Artery: CO2 high, O2 low; vein: CO2 low, O2 high","wrong":["Artery: CO2 low, O2 high; vein: CO2 high, O2 low","Artery: CO2 high, O2 high; vein: CO2 low, O2 low","Artery: CO2 high, O2 low; vein: CO2 high, O2 low"],"why":"Two points: the artery brings blood from the body (CO2 high, O2 low); the vein brings it from the lungs (CO2 low, O2 high)."},{"q":"Which axes should your graph of the table have? (one of 4 points)","right":"x: time (min); y: blood pH","wrong":["x: blood pH; y: time (min)","x: person (A or B); y: blood pH","x: time (min); y: person (A or B)"],"why":"Time on x, blood pH on y. All 4 points: labelled axes, even scales, every point plotted, and two lines labelled Person A and Person B."},{"q":"Who is better at removing CO2 from the blood, and why? (3 points)","right":"A: pH stays normal, B's drops, so less CO2 builds up in A","wrong":["B: pH drops to 7.17, so less CO2 builds up in B's blood","A: pH rises in exercise, so A's cells make less CO2","B: blood gets more acidic, so B removes CO2 faster"],"why":"Three points: person A; A's pH stays normal (7.35) while B's falls to 7.17; so less CO2 builds up in A, who removes it faster."}],"S1-Q3d":[{"q":"Which muscles cause inhalation, and are they contracting or relaxing?","right":"Diaphragm and intercostals, both contracting","wrong":["Diaphragm and intercostals, both relaxing","Diaphragm contracting, intercostals relaxing","Diaphragm relaxing, intercostals contracting"],"why":"Both muscles contract to breathe in: the diaphragm flattens and the intercostals lift the ribs."},{"q":"From the graph, roughly how much air goes in and out per breath at rest?","right":"About 0.5 L","wrong":["About 2.0 L","About 2.5 L","About 3.5 L"],"why":"At rest the trace goes from 2.0 L up to 2.5 L each breath, so about 0.5 L moves in and out."},{"q":"Where on the graph should you write X, the start of exercise?","right":"Where breaths start getting faster and deeper","wrong":["Where breaths return to slow and shallow","At the tallest peak of the trace","At the very start of the graph"],"why":"Exercise starts where the trace changes from slow, small waves to quick, big ones (about 15 s)."},{"q":"From the graph, how did the athlete's breathing change during exercise?","right":"Breaths got faster and deeper","wrong":["Breaths got faster but shallower","Breaths got slower but deeper","Breaths got slower and shallower"],"why":"The waves come closer together (higher rate) and get taller (more air per breath) during exercise."}],"S2-Q5a":[{"q":"How does the CO2 level in the plasma change from vessel A to vessel B? (3 points)","right":"A low (just left lungs); B high (cells add CO2); so it rises","wrong":["A high (from body cells); B low (lungs removed it); it falls","A low (just left lungs); B low (cells use it up); no change","A high (lungs add CO2); B higher (body adds more); it rises"],"why":"Three points: A (aorta) is low in CO2 as it just left the lungs; B (pulmonary artery) is high as body cells added CO2; so it rises."}]};
